To insert data into a table using an Express controller, you'll need to create a route that handles the request and uses TypeORM to interact with the database.

Here's an example of how you can create a simple controller to insert data into the "user" table:
```
// src/controller/UserController.ts
import { Controller, Post, Body } from 'express';
import { DataSource } from 'typeorm';
import { User } from '../entity/User';

@Controller('users')
export class UserController {
  @Post('create')
  async createUser(@Body() userData: User) {
    const dataSource = new DataSource();
    const userRepository = dataSource.getRepository(User);
    const user = new User();
    user.fname = userData.fname;
    user.lname = userData.lname;
    user.email = userData.email;
    user.phno = userData.phno;
    await userRepository.save(user);
    return { message: 'User created successfully' };
  }
}
```
In this example, we've created a `UserController` with a `createUser` method that handles the request to create a new user. The method uses the `DataSource` and `getRepository` methods to get a reference to the `User` repository, and then creates a new `User` entity and saves it to the database using the `save` method.

You can then use this controller in your Express routes like this:
```
// src/index.ts
import { UserController } from './controller/UserController';
import express from 'express';

const app = express();
const userController = new UserController();

app.post('/users/create', userController.createUser);
```
This will create a new route that handles POST requests to `/users/create` and calls the `createUser` method on the `UserController` instance.

Note that this is just a simple example, and you may need to add additional logic and error handling depending on your specific use case.
